  "task2_latency_non_equivalence_audit": {
    "verdict": "NON-EQUIVALENT TIMING PROTOCOLS \u2014 DO NOT CLAIM DIRECT SPEEDUP OVER SELFCHECKGPT FROM THESE TWO SEPARATE MEASUREMENTS",
    "exact_causes_of_discrepancy": [
      "1. Attention kernel mismatch: TRUEGUARD latency (real_latency.json) used attn_implementation='eager' (required for attention weights), whereas Step 3 M=10 sampling used PyTorch's fast 'sdpa' kernel.",
      "2. Forced token length mismatch: real_latency.json forced min_new_tokens=24 (always decoding all 24 tokens), whereas Step 3 M=10 sampling allowed early EOS stopping (actual mean answer length ~8-12 words).",
      "3. Batch size mismatch: real_latency.json ran sequential batch_size=1 (simulating high-concurrency/streaming serving), whereas Step 3 batched all M=10 samples in parallel (batch_size=10) on an otherwise idle T4 GPU.",
      "4. Missing initial greedy generation in SelfCheckGPT total: external_baselines_report.json logged 0.7068 s/q for 10 extra samples + NLI without adding the initial greedy generation (~0.715 s/q vanilla under forced-24 eager decode).",
      "5. Scope boundary: TRUEGUARD monitoring-only latency excludes Tier 1 resampling and Tier 2 Wikipedia retrieval mitigation actions."
    ],
    "fair_claim_supported": "TRUEGUARD achieves 4.20x-4.60x speedup over sequential 5-sample decoding under identical conditions (real_latency.json: same model, eager kernel, batch_size=1, 24 forced tokens), and requires 1 LLM generation and 0 external NLI models (vs. 10-11 generations + a 435M DeBERTa-large model requiring 1.00-2.47 GB extra VRAM for SelfCheckGPT-NLI and Semantic Entropy)."
  },
